  "question": {
    "text": "The circumcentre of a triangle lies at the origin and its centroid is the midpoint of the line segment joining the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>,&#160;-&#160;2</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#8800;0</mo></math>.&nbsp;Then for any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;the orthocentre of this triangle lies on the line",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "tf4p5",
